  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nThe main story through the mid-week period continues to lie with\na couple more upper level shortwave disturbances passing \nthrough...overall there hasn't been any notable changes in the \nmodels/forecast. \n\nThink that Wednesday will be similar to today...in that there\ncould be some activity left over from tonight lingering around\nmainly southern/eastern portions of the area during the early-\nmid morning hours...with the majority of the daytime hours being\ndry. Not a lot of change in the upper level pattern, with the \npersistent ridging keeping that west-northwesterly flow in \nplace. At the surface, models are showing winds turning more \nsoutheasterly...with speeds around 10-15 MPH, some show the \npotential for gusts near 20 MPH in spots. Forecast highs also \nsimilar to today in the upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nLittle more uncertainty remains Wednesday night on through\nThursday night with the timing/coverage of precipitation \nassociated with additional disturbances moving through. Models \nvary more on Wed night into Thursday, some more generous with \nactivity, others showing a smaller swath developing with weaker \nforcing. Models remain pretty consistent showing a stronger wave\nmoving through Thu evening-night...bringing better potential \nfor storms, some of which could be strong-severe (the entire \narea remains in the SPC Day 3 Marginal Risk area). Forecast \nhighs a bit more of a range from the mid 80s-mid 90s...driven by\nsome models keeping activity lingering around longer into the \nday than others. \n\nFriday on into early next week...\n\nForecast for the end of the week into the start of the new work\nweek dries back out. The main impact from the disturbance \nmoving through Thursday night is with the westward shift of the \nmain upper level ridge axis, bringing more amplified north-\nnorthwesterly flow to the Central Plains at least through the \nweekend. Models showing an overall lack of disturbances through \nthe weekend, with some low PoPs returning around Tuesday. Breezy\nNNE winds are forecast for Friday in the wake of a sfc frontal \npassage...but at this point that front doesn't look to have a \nnotable downward impact on temps or dewpoints, with highs in the\nmid-80s to near 90. Forecast highs through the weekend on into \nMonday are similar to Friday, mainly in the 80s.",
